-- Q4214: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 351(b), when boot is received in a Sec. 351 exchange, gain is recognized to the extent of boot but not exceeding realized gain. Tom''s realized gain is $60,000 ($100,000 FMV minus $40,000 basis). The $15,000 boot is less than realized gain, so Tom recognizes $15,000.
Wrong (A): $0 gain applies only when no boot is received; here Tom received $15,000 cash boot triggering gain recognition under IRC Sec. 351(b).
Wrong (B): $60,000 is the total realized gain, but recognized gain is limited to the lesser of boot received ($15,000) or realized gain ($60,000).
Wrong (D): $40,000 is the property''s adjusted basis, not a recognized gain amount under IRC Sec. 351(b).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4214;

-- Q4215: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 358(a)(1), the transferor''s basis in stock received in a Sec. 351 exchange equals the adjusted basis of property transferred ($70,000) plus gain recognized ($20,000) minus boot received ($20,000) = $70,000. The substituted basis preserves deferred gain for future recognition.
Wrong (B): $90,000 incorrectly adds recognized gain without subtracting boot, or adds boot to basis; both approaches misapply Sec. 358(a).
Wrong (C): $110,000 is FMV of the transferred property; stock basis uses the Sec. 358 substituted basis formula, not FMV.
Wrong (D): $50,000 incorrectly subtracts boot without adding recognized gain, understating the stock basis under Sec. 358(a).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4215;

-- Q4216: Entity Formation and Liquidation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Sec. 351 requires that the transferors as a group control the corporation immediately after the exchange. Control is defined under IRC Sec. 368(c) as ownership of at least 80% of total combined voting power and at least 80% of each class of nonvoting stock.
Wrong (A): 50% is not sufficient; IRC Sec. 368(c) sets the control threshold at 80%, not a simple majority.
Wrong (B): 51% is a simple majority threshold used in other contexts, but IRC Sec. 368(c) requires 80% for Sec. 351 purposes.
Wrong (D): 100% ownership is not required; IRC Sec. 351 only requires 80% control, allowing up to 20% to be held by non-transferors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4216;

-- Q4218: Entity Formation and Liquidation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 357(a), the assumption of liabilities by the corporation is generally not treated as boot in a Sec. 351 exchange. Since the $10,000 liability does not exceed the transferor''s $30,000 basis, no gain is recognized. The shareholder''s stock basis is reduced to $20,000 ($30,000 minus $10,000 liability).
Wrong (A): $20,000 is the resulting stock basis, not recognized gain; IRC Sec. 357(a) excludes the liability assumption from boot treatment.
Wrong (B): $10,000 would be gain if the liability were treated as boot, but IRC Sec. 357(a) provides that liability assumptions are not boot in a qualifying exchange.
Wrong (D): $50,000 is the FMV of the property, not a recognized gain amount; no gain is recognized under IRC Sec. 351 with Sec. 357(a) applying.$EXPL$
